<p>I was on my Mac this morning, just minding my own business, when suddenly I found myself using the Linux laptop, and I liked it.</p>
<!-- more -->
<p>On Sunday mornings, I do system maintenance. This means running updates on all my computers. Right now, that's five machines: one Linux desktop, two Linux laptops, one Mac desktop, and one Mac laptop. Yes, that's too many, but it is what it is.</p>
<p>Both Linux laptops run <a href="https://cachyos.org/">CachyOS</a>, which is Arch-based, so there are <em>always</em> updates.</p>
<p>I started with the <a href="https://frame.work/">Framework</a>. Once it was updated and rebooted, I pulled the latest tweaks to my Emacs config, to make sure it worked on Linux. I've been <a href="https://baty.net/posts/2026/09/i-am-comfortable-living-in-linux-but/">leaning toward using macOS exclusively</a>, so I haven't been testing things on Linux as much. Everything worked, though.</p>
<p>While typing on the Framework, the first thing I thought about was how much I prefer typing on it to typing on the MacBook Air. Then I remembered how much I like the <em>idea</em> of Linux. Then I noticed that Emacs starts up more than twice as fast on the Framework for some reason. Then I remembered that the best (and only) way to have consistent Emacs key bindings on Linux is to, well, use Emacs.</p>
<p>All this to say that the conflict between running macOS, Linux, or both is still a thing. As troublesome and complex as it can be, I think the answer will always be &quot;both&quot;. Surely I can manage it, right? Maybe if I only use the laptops for simple things like blogging, browsing, or journaling, things will be easy enough. I'll never be able to settle on one or the other. Just admit it.</p>
<p>I'm on the couch typing this in Emacs using the Framework, both things I told myself a week ago that I'd stop doing. My stupid brain is always mucking things up.</p>
